# dev bundle — config ownership (deploy.sh SDK + w-reset).
# TSV: <path>\t<class>. Absolute path → config/root<path> (always managed);
# home-relative → config/skel/<path>. class: user = seed-if-absent, managed =
# overwrite (W owns it). A trailing '/' marks a directory tree.
#
# NOTE: three themed files are deliberately NOT listed here — they are owned by the
# bundle's w-style axes and re-rendered on every theme switch (listing them would
# mean two writers for one file):
#   ~/.config/zed/themes/w.json    → axis 400-zed
#   ~/.config/w/git-theme.conf     → axis 500-git
#   ~/.config/w/lazygit-theme.yml  → axis 500-lazygit
# Same split as helix (config.toml user / themes/w.toml w-style) and micro.

# Zed user settings: selects the "W" theme, W fonts, privacy/update policy and
# wires w-mcp (MCP) + goose (ACP) into the editor's agent panel. User-seed: the
# single settings file is also where the user's own preferences live, so W writes
# it once and never again (update-system.md bucket C, like helix/micro/btop).
.config/zed/settings.json	user

# Git — layered, so W can ship changes without ever touching the user's file. Git
# reads BOTH global configs: ~/.config/git/config first, ~/.gitconfig second, later
# wins. So W takes the first (managed: delta wiring, the theme [include], defaults)
# and leaves the second entirely to the user, who thereby overrides W key by key.
#
# The ~/.gitconfig stub is seeded even though it carries no settings: `git config
# --global` writes to it ONLY if it exists — otherwise git would write the user's
# identity into W's managed file, and the next update would erase it.
#
# git re-reads its config on every invocation, so a `w-theme set` is visible in the
# very next `git diff`.
.config/git/config	managed
.gitconfig	user

# lazygit behaviour (delta as the pager). Colours live in the axis-rendered
# ~/.config/w/lazygit-theme.yml; the session env below merges the two, so this file
# stays 100% the user's.
.config/lazygit/config.yml	user

# mise settings: the uv/mise boundary (uv owns Python venvs) and nothing else. The
# tool list itself (`mise use -g …`) is written here by mise on the user's command,
# which is exactly why this is a user-seed and not managed.
.config/mise/config.toml	user

# Session env: LG_CONFIG_FILE layers the user config + the W theme file for lazygit
# (lazygit's own documented comma-separated merge), and mise's shim dir goes on PATH
# so GUI apps launched from Hyprland — Zed and its language servers above all — see
# the project toolchain. Sourced by env-hyprland at graphical-session-pre → active
# from the next login.
/etc/w/env.d/dev.sh	managed

# Interactive-shell hook: `mise activate zsh` (per-prompt PATH, the mode upstream
# recommends for interactive use). Sourced by the /etc/w/zshrc.d drop-in loop in
# .zshrc → active in the next NEW shell.
/etc/w/zshrc.d/dev.zsh	managed
